course: dictionary
lesson: 11004
title: Wasting: losing or using up for nothing
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Wasting is the -ing form of the verb waste. Its meaning depends on the words around it. «Wasting time» means spending time in a way you later wish you hadn't. Check the context: if you could have done something better with that time, it is wasting.
explain: Wasting can also mean using more than you need. Look for clues like «left on» or «more than you need». If the sentence describes a lot being used for no reason, wasting means using up something that could have been saved.
explain: Context matters for fairness too. Resting, chilling or playing are not wasting if you chose them. Wasting only fits when you later wish you had spent your time or energy differently.
explain: Wasting can also describe an illness, as in «a wasting illness». The clues are illness and body: wasting means the body grows thin and weak. Swap in your meaning and check it still makes sense.
fact: Wasting is the -ing form of the verb waste: waste, wastes, wasted, wasting.
fact: «Wasting» means using something for nothing, or losing it, such as time, water, food or money.
fact: «Wasting» also means using more than you need.
fact: A wasting illness makes the body grow thin and weak.
fact: «Wasting time» means spending it in a way you later wish you hadn't.
exercise: Mum says, «Stop wasting your breath, nobody is listening.» What does wasting your breath mean? | Blowing up a balloon | Breathing very fast while running | Singing loudly | *Talking for nothing
exercise: «Lights left on in an empty room are wasting energy.» What is being used up for nothing? | The room | *Energy | The sunlight | Empty chairs
exercise: The word wasting in «a wasting illness» means: | *Making the body grow thin and weak | Going very fast | Being very rich | Eating too much at once
test: Is resting you chose to do a waste of time? | Yes, if it is after lunch | Yes, always | *No, it is not waste if you chose it | Only on Sundays
test: Read: «Wasting is a quiet wrong: nobody cries at once.» What does quiet wrong suggest? | A loud argument | A good thing done quickly | A sound you can hear clearly | *Something bad that happens slowly, without anyone noticing at first
exam: Which sentence uses wasting to mean using more than you need? | Taking one biscuit from the tin | Walking to school in the morning | *Filling a bowl with far more cereal than you can eat | Sharing sweets with a friend
